WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.406 --> 00:00:01.701
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.175
We put out a lot of content,

00:00:03.175 --> 00:00:05.582
and I often get asked:
can you summarize it down

00:00:05.582 --> 00:00:07.156
to some of your favorite clips?

00:00:07.156 --> 00:00:09.761
And so that's what this
video today is all about.

00:00:09.761 --> 00:00:13.307
Some of my most favorite
rules from the top 10 series,

00:00:13.307 --> 00:00:15.307
February, 2017, edition.

00:00:16.784 --> 00:00:17.707
I hope you enjoy.

00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:19.519
And as always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.477
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:21.477 --> 00:00:23.909
leave it down in the comments
below, put quotes around it

00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:25.870
so other people can be inspired as well,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.977
and when you write it
down, it's much more likely

00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:31.245
to sink in for yourself, as well.

00:00:31.245 --> 00:00:32.078
Enjoy.

00:00:35.266 --> 00:00:38.404
- You are unusually
fearless, and willing to go

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.328
in the face of other people
telling you something

00:00:40.328 --> 00:00:42.152
is crazy, and I know a lot of pretty

00:00:42.152 --> 00:00:43.485
crazy people, you still stand out.

00:00:43.485 --> 00:00:46.093
Where does that come from, or
how do you think about making

00:00:46.093 --> 00:00:48.521
a decision when everyone tells
you: this is a crazy idea.

00:00:48.521 --> 00:00:51.401
Or where do you get the
internal strength to do that?

00:00:51.401 --> 00:00:53.291
- Well, first of all, I
would say I actually think I,

00:00:53.291 --> 00:00:56.041
think I feel fear quite strongly.

00:00:57.202 --> 00:01:00.241
So it's not as though I just
have the absence of fear.

00:01:00.241 --> 00:01:03.144
I feel it quite strongly.

00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:05.884
But there are just times when something

00:01:05.884 --> 00:01:08.154
is important enough,
you believe in it enough

00:01:08.154 --> 00:01:11.450
that you do it in spite of the fear.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:12.779
- So, speaking of important things--

00:01:12.779 --> 00:01:14.547
- Like, people shouldn't think I,

00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:17.841
people shouldn't think,
well, I feel fear about this,

00:01:17.841 --> 00:01:19.762
and therefore I shouldn't do it.

00:01:19.762 --> 00:01:22.291
It's normal to feel fear.

00:01:22.291 --> 00:01:24.569
Like, you'd have to, definitely
something mentally wrong

00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:26.569
if you didn't feel fear.

00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:29.391
- So you just feel it
and let the importance

00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:31.643
of it drive you to do it anyway?

00:01:31.643 --> 00:01:33.872
- Yeah, you know, actually
something that can be helpful

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:36.608
is fatalism, to some degree.

00:01:36.608 --> 00:01:38.245
If you just accept the probabilities,

00:01:38.245 --> 00:01:40.412
then that diminishes fear.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.163
So, when starting SpaceX,
I thought the odds

00:01:46.163 --> 00:01:49.999
of success were less than 10 percent.

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:51.597
And I just accepted that, actually,

00:01:51.597 --> 00:01:54.764
probably I would just lose everything.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:58.656
But that maybe we would
make some progress,

00:01:58.656 --> 00:02:00.294
if we could just move the ball forward,

00:02:00.294 --> 00:02:03.005
even if we died, maybe some other company

00:02:03.005 --> 00:02:06.949
could pick up the baton
and keep moving it forward.

00:02:06.949 --> 00:02:09.331
So that we'd still do some good.

00:02:09.331 --> 00:02:11.324
Yeah, same with Tesla.

00:02:11.324 --> 00:02:13.017
I thought the odds of a car company

00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:15.517
succeeding were extremely low.

00:02:17.339 --> 00:02:21.506
- If we could celebrate
every breath that we take,

00:02:23.224 --> 00:02:25.796
because nothing's a guarantee.

00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:29.796
If we could celebrate the
breath and the exhale,

00:02:31.212 --> 00:02:34.545
which is being alive, without, you know,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.323
ah, my car won't start,
oh, geez, you know,

00:02:40.323 --> 00:02:42.009
the cat dropped dead.

00:02:42.009 --> 00:02:44.492
(laughter)

00:02:44.492 --> 00:02:48.159
Hey, I, you know, I
think I need a lobotomy.

00:02:49.552 --> 00:02:52.917
You know, I mean, there
are things that are very,

00:02:52.917 --> 00:02:55.380
very important in life that we,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.732
we just don't think about, you know?

00:02:58.732 --> 00:03:02.112
And one of the most worrisome
things that I went through,

00:03:02.112 --> 00:03:06.279
through years, is you're
freaked out about your past,

00:03:08.377 --> 00:03:11.938
you're worried, you're scared
to death of the future,

00:03:11.938 --> 00:03:13.416
and that's all you're thinking about.

00:03:13.416 --> 00:03:16.384
But the now doesn't exist, you know?

00:03:16.384 --> 00:03:19.248
And I think that's a grave
mistake that we make.

00:03:19.248 --> 00:03:23.529
- I've always had this really
gross, dangerous mentality

00:03:23.529 --> 00:03:26.862
of the, ah, no consideration of failure.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.451
Just never even considering
the thought of failing.

00:03:30.451 --> 00:03:34.618
Like, if I want something,
I just go until I get it.

00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:40.301
And that doesn't always work, but.

00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:43.176
- I believe that the depth
of anybody's struggle

00:03:43.176 --> 00:03:45.643
will determine the
height of their success.

00:03:45.643 --> 00:03:47.822
And I believe that everything
that I've gone through

00:03:47.822 --> 00:03:50.361
in my whole life, you know, people ask me:

00:03:50.361 --> 00:03:51.658
what would you change?

00:03:51.658 --> 00:03:53.737
I wouldn't change a
thing, because everything

00:03:53.737 --> 00:03:56.878
that I've gone through has
made me who I am today.

00:03:56.878 --> 00:03:58.906
You know, so I'm sitting here very strong

00:03:58.906 --> 00:04:00.928
and very confident, you know?

00:04:00.928 --> 00:04:04.109
And got a lot of love for
a lot, a lot of people.

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:06.059
Jesus struggled and went
through things, you know?

00:04:06.059 --> 00:04:08.014
Everybody struggled and went
through their own thing,

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.661
and people slanted 'em.

00:04:10.661 --> 00:04:13.399
I mean, I feel like if Jesus
can go what he went through,

00:04:13.399 --> 00:04:16.506
who am I? (laughing)

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:19.480
I'm nobody; just a guy
from the 'hood, man.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.610
Just, that just do this music, you know?

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:23.763
But I'm just such a real person,

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.779
I don't consider myself
a celebrity at all.

00:04:25.779 --> 00:04:27.598
I'm just so real, and I love people that,

00:04:27.598 --> 00:04:30.189
you know, man, I just
flow through this thing.

00:04:30.189 --> 00:04:32.382
And I let my music and I let God guide me,

00:04:32.382 --> 00:04:33.738
where he guide me to go.

00:04:33.738 --> 00:04:35.859
But God ain't going to just
guide you straight to success.

00:04:35.859 --> 00:04:37.244
Not God.

00:04:37.244 --> 00:04:39.996
He going to guide you
through a lot of mess, first.

00:04:39.996 --> 00:04:41.457
So that when you get the success,

00:04:41.457 --> 00:04:42.803
you'll know how to appreciate it,

00:04:42.803 --> 00:04:44.463
you'll know how to stay with it,

00:04:44.463 --> 00:04:45.882
and know how to take care of it.

00:04:45.882 --> 00:04:48.624
And that's called: longevity.

00:04:48.624 --> 00:04:51.279
- I'm a very spiritual person
and I follow my spirit.

00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:54.262
And I do what my spirit tells me to do.

00:04:54.262 --> 00:04:56.618
You know, and I don't have set, like,

00:04:56.618 --> 00:04:57.984
oh, I want to play this type of character

00:04:57.984 --> 00:04:59.934
and I won't play that type of character.

00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:03.510
I'm receptive to anything, you know?

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.702
And when the right thing comes along,

00:05:05.702 --> 00:05:07.614
if it's serious, if it's funny,

00:05:07.614 --> 00:05:10.457
if it's silly, if it's low, but see,

00:05:10.457 --> 00:05:15.120
put it this way: I do,
just do whatever makes me,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.541
I get the most gratification
from, as an artist.

00:05:19.541 --> 00:05:22.572
I do music albums and
nobody buys my records.

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:25.198
I do them because I love
making music, you know?

00:05:25.198 --> 00:05:27.092
And that's the same way I am about movies.

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:29.363
I don't make movies because I,

00:05:29.363 --> 00:05:31.387
I don't go into it
thinking: let me do this,

00:05:31.387 --> 00:05:32.901
because then people will come.

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:34.269
Or: how much will a movie make?

00:05:34.269 --> 00:05:36.215
And: I'm trying to make
the audience do that.

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:38.876
I do what feels right for me.

00:05:38.876 --> 00:05:41.196
- What's your message
for viewers out there

00:05:41.196 --> 00:05:42.442
about following their dreams.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:46.216
I would say, ah, if you
truly believe in something

00:05:46.216 --> 00:05:48.179
and you really have a
passion for something,

00:05:48.179 --> 00:05:49.545
then go for it.

00:05:49.545 --> 00:05:51.540
I think there's a lot of
obstacles that you have

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:54.443
to go through and nothing's
ever given to you,

00:05:54.443 --> 00:05:56.444
and it takes a lot of
hard work and dedication,

00:05:56.444 --> 00:05:58.868
but if you really believe in
it, then you should do it.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:01.451
(piano melody)

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:04.987
- It started in the crib; I was a baby.

00:06:04.987 --> 00:06:09.453
And from the cradle, all the
way to age 11, more or less,

00:06:09.453 --> 00:06:12.382
I had what is called "lucid
dreaming", which means

00:06:12.382 --> 00:06:15.835
that you would dream that you are awake.

00:06:15.835 --> 00:06:17.394
- Hola?

00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:19.977
- So, I literally saw monsters.

00:06:21.974 --> 00:06:24.091
I was used to monsters, I loved them.

00:06:24.091 --> 00:06:27.773
But at a very early age
I made a deal with them

00:06:27.773 --> 00:06:30.072
to allow me to go to the bathroom,

00:06:30.072 --> 00:06:34.857
because I was so afraid,
I ended up peeing my crib.

00:06:34.857 --> 00:06:37.524
And you know, we stayed friends.

00:06:40.201 --> 00:06:42.698
You know, there are only
two things you can do in art

00:06:42.698 --> 00:06:44.377
or in narrative, in storytelling.

00:06:44.377 --> 00:06:47.218
You can tell about the good stuff in life,

00:06:47.218 --> 00:06:49.973
which has always been very boring to me,

00:06:49.973 --> 00:06:53.178
and you can tell stories
about the dark side of life,

00:06:53.178 --> 00:06:56.016
which has been much more attractive to me.

00:06:56.016 --> 00:06:59.111
When I talk to pre-first-time filmmakers,

00:06:59.111 --> 00:07:01.959
or first-time filmmakers,
I always tell them:

00:07:01.959 --> 00:07:06.126
look, making movies is eating
a sandwich of (audio deleted).

00:07:08.015 --> 00:07:10.144
And the only thing that
gets a little better is,

00:07:10.144 --> 00:07:13.207
as the years go by, you
get a little more bread.

00:07:13.207 --> 00:07:15.814
But the (audio deleted) is always there.

00:07:15.814 --> 00:07:17.725
- [Interviewer] But isn't
that true of life, also?

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:19.023
- I think it is.

00:07:19.023 --> 00:07:23.037
I mean, I use another
metaphor for life. (laughing)

00:07:23.037 --> 00:07:26.088
But for film, I tell you, that one fits.

00:07:26.088 --> 00:07:29.742
- [Interviewer] But in the
end, somehow, you find the joy?

00:07:29.742 --> 00:07:32.336
- Well, you end up convincing yourself

00:07:32.336 --> 00:07:37.335
that it's peanut butter. (laughing)

00:07:37.335 --> 00:07:39.587
- [Interviewer] And is
it ever peanut butter?

00:07:39.587 --> 00:07:43.689
- I want to believe it is. (laughing)

00:07:43.689 --> 00:07:45.564
- [Interviewer] But do
you look back and go:

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:47.362
yes, I did that, okay.

00:07:47.362 --> 00:07:50.995
- I tell you, without a
doubt, we may say this

00:07:50.995 --> 00:07:55.162
is the greatest, being an
artist in whatever discipline,

00:07:56.756 --> 00:08:00.216
dance, painting, theater,
whatever you want,

00:08:00.216 --> 00:08:02.727
is the greatest work on Earth.

00:08:02.727 --> 00:08:04.515
I mean, it's truly this hard thing,

00:08:04.515 --> 00:08:08.205
in this way or that way,
but so is every job.

00:08:08.205 --> 00:08:09.409
So is every job.

00:08:09.409 --> 00:08:13.419
I, when I was younger,
I worked with my father

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:16.286
on real estate for about five years.

00:08:16.286 --> 00:08:18.711
I was the worst car salesman

00:08:18.711 --> 00:08:22.017
in the history of car salesmanship.

00:08:22.017 --> 00:08:23.039
- [Interviewer] Because
you were too honest?

00:08:23.039 --> 00:08:25.098
- Oh, yeah, people would come
and say: what about this?

00:08:25.098 --> 00:08:27.134
I think, ah, no. (laughing)

00:08:27.134 --> 00:08:28.771
I was, you know, Glengarry Glen Ross,

00:08:28.771 --> 00:08:31.454
I would not get the knife set.

00:08:31.454 --> 00:08:34.405
I would be Shelley Levine.

00:08:34.405 --> 00:08:37.569
I would be out, out; it was terrible.

00:08:37.569 --> 00:08:40.500
My father said: you're terrible!

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:44.726
And the other vendors,
whenever I went out,

00:08:44.726 --> 00:08:47.622
the other salesmen, whenever
I went out to a client,

00:08:47.622 --> 00:08:49.727
they actually laughed behind my back.

00:08:49.727 --> 00:08:53.291
Actually, physically were
laughing behind my back.

00:08:53.291 --> 00:08:54.657
And I was the worst.

00:08:54.657 --> 00:08:56.116
But in real estate, I was good.

00:08:56.116 --> 00:08:57.486
I was good at real estate.

00:08:57.486 --> 00:08:59.959
And my father used to
say, you have to do this

00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:02.399
to show that you can
have an honest living,

00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:04.628
do an honest living,
and what I did was work

00:09:04.628 --> 00:09:05.963
on real estate in the mornings,

00:09:05.963 --> 00:09:07.995
and I did monsters in the afternoon.

00:09:07.995 --> 00:09:10.443
But I know that when you have a real job,

00:09:10.443 --> 00:09:14.636
I painted highways one
summer, in Wichita, Kansas.

00:09:14.636 --> 00:09:19.081
I mean, a real job has equal
heartbreak and stuff like that.

00:09:19.081 --> 00:09:20.588
And politics.

00:09:20.588 --> 00:09:21.836
- [Interviewer] Well, I mean, this is,

00:09:21.836 --> 00:09:23.471
I think it's one of the
things that people really

00:09:23.471 --> 00:09:26.174
misunderstand, is as
glorious as it is to be able

00:09:26.174 --> 00:09:27.881
to do the things you're passionate about

00:09:27.881 --> 00:09:30.164
and to be an artist, it's
still a job; it is not--

00:09:30.164 --> 00:09:31.491
- Oh, it's still a job, yeah.

00:09:31.491 --> 00:09:32.367
- [Interviewer] It is work.

00:09:32.367 --> 00:09:36.798
- Yeah, yeah, it is, but God
bless anyone that can do this.

00:09:36.798 --> 00:09:40.428
It's beautiful, and you
have to truly be grateful.

00:09:40.428 --> 00:09:43.418
And the only way to remain
objective about how grateful

00:09:43.418 --> 00:09:47.585
you need to be, is present
your present to your past self,

00:09:49.856 --> 00:09:52.505
when you were 20 years old, and if you go

00:09:52.505 --> 00:09:54.743
and talk to yourself at
20 years old and said:

00:09:54.743 --> 00:09:57.835
would you like to be doing this movie?

00:09:57.835 --> 00:09:59.507
With this budget?

00:09:59.507 --> 00:10:02.437
What do you think your
response at age 20 would be?

00:10:02.437 --> 00:10:05.357
Abso-(audio deleted)-lutely,
yes, you know?

00:10:05.357 --> 00:10:09.513
- Some jobs you learn by going
to college, and they give you

00:10:09.513 --> 00:10:11.645
an education and you
bring that to your work.

00:10:11.645 --> 00:10:14.933
And some jobs you learn
by just being on the job.

00:10:14.933 --> 00:10:18.944
And from Cursed Part Three
on, all the way up to

00:10:18.944 --> 00:10:21.929
Jurassic World, my knowledge
of how to be on a set,

00:10:21.929 --> 00:10:25.942
how to act, and how to
handle myself on set

00:10:25.942 --> 00:10:28.553
and approach work is continuing to grow.

00:10:28.553 --> 00:10:29.966
I've learned a lot on every job.

00:10:29.966 --> 00:10:32.931
It would be smart, when
on a job, to be a sponge.

00:10:32.931 --> 00:10:34.419
Pick somebody and learn from them.

00:10:34.419 --> 00:10:35.967
I'm getting to the point now where

00:10:35.967 --> 00:10:38.302
I have more experience than
a lot of the people that are

00:10:38.302 --> 00:10:40.374
on the set, and I'm
actually at the point where

00:10:40.374 --> 00:10:42.365
I can teach other people,
so that's pretty cool, too.

00:10:42.365 --> 00:10:46.176
- I enjoy doing them, but more than that,

00:10:46.176 --> 00:10:47.946
actually, let me rephrase that.

00:10:47.946 --> 00:10:50.770
I think the reason why I
enjoy it so much is because

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.428
how much the people get off,

00:10:52.428 --> 00:10:55.110
the audience appreciates what we do.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.105
You know, there's a lot
of things I could be doing

00:10:57.105 --> 00:11:00.386
outside of the industry,
even within the industry.

00:11:00.386 --> 00:11:03.478
But, you know, I don't know if the

00:11:03.478 --> 00:11:05.762
satisfaction would be the same.

00:11:05.762 --> 00:11:07.892
You know, because to
be a part of something

00:11:07.892 --> 00:11:10.998
that people really appreciate,

00:11:10.998 --> 00:11:13.233
as much as maybe certain
people hate to admit it,

00:11:13.233 --> 00:11:15.343
it's important to us, as humans.

00:11:15.343 --> 00:11:17.182
It's like you want to
believe that, you know,

00:11:17.182 --> 00:11:18.412
what you're putting out there

00:11:18.412 --> 00:11:20.080
and what you've dedicated
so much time to is,

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:22.709
there's a certain degree
of acknowledgement,

00:11:22.709 --> 00:11:25.871
and more than
acknowledgement, appreciation.

00:11:25.871 --> 00:11:29.341
And obviously, this one's been
appreciated for a long time.

00:11:29.341 --> 00:11:30.945
And that's a great feeling.

00:11:30.945 --> 00:11:35.372
- If it's in your heart,
you have to push for it.

00:11:35.372 --> 00:11:36.539
If it's in your heart.

00:11:36.539 --> 00:11:38.286
Because I'm from Kansas City, Missouri.

00:11:38.286 --> 00:11:41.774
Who would ever thought that
you can build an empire?

00:11:41.774 --> 00:11:45.093
But if you put your mind
to it and push for it,

00:11:45.093 --> 00:11:47.510
and push for it and keep,
no matter if your family

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:49.608
tells you it's a hobby,

00:11:49.608 --> 00:11:52.419
you need to do something else
to get money or whatever, man,

00:11:52.419 --> 00:11:54.666
it's like, if you believe
in it, you got to follow

00:11:54.666 --> 00:11:57.748
that dream in your heart,
man, because if I didn't,

00:11:57.748 --> 00:12:00.786
there wouldn't be any strange music

00:12:00.786 --> 00:12:02.893
to come do any interview in.

00:12:02.893 --> 00:12:07.191
Because I followed my dream
after everybody knocked me down.

00:12:07.191 --> 00:12:08.220
You have to push for it.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.324
If you believe in it, you will achieve it.

00:12:10.324 --> 00:12:11.766
You just got to keep pushing.

00:12:11.766 --> 00:12:13.238
That's what I have to say.

00:12:13.238 --> 00:12:15.072
- I look towards the future.

00:12:15.072 --> 00:12:18.737
And the future is just,
you know, it's more to do.

00:12:18.737 --> 00:12:21.654
It's more steps to climb and stuff.

00:12:22.622 --> 00:12:25.472
So I don't really look at
what I'm doing right now,

00:12:25.472 --> 00:12:27.495
because every time I do
something, I move on,

00:12:27.495 --> 00:12:30.211
and see how could I get better,
and I'm never satisfied.

00:12:30.211 --> 00:12:32.113
You know, I think I always
know I could do better.

00:12:32.113 --> 00:12:33.395
- Always knew you could do better?

00:12:33.395 --> 00:12:34.705
- I could do better, yeah.

00:12:34.705 --> 00:12:36.402
- I know there's so many kids out there

00:12:36.402 --> 00:12:38.762
that feel the way that
I did when I was a kid.

00:12:38.762 --> 00:12:42.843
Just awkward and not
excelling at one thing,

00:12:42.843 --> 00:12:44.916
in particular, and not cool.

00:12:44.916 --> 00:12:48.460
And skateboarding was my way out of that.

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:51.878
And I hope that, if
not with skateboarding,

00:12:51.878 --> 00:12:53.929
they find it in something else they do.

00:12:53.929 --> 00:12:55.225
But I hope that they're not afraid

00:12:55.225 --> 00:12:56.522
to try something different.

00:12:56.522 --> 00:12:58.250
Because you can't listen to the haters.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:00.101
That's my best advice to anyone.

00:13:00.101 --> 00:13:01.613
If I had listened to the haters,

00:13:01.613 --> 00:13:03.865
I would have quit a long time ago.

00:13:03.865 --> 00:13:06.328
I would have quit when I was 11 years old.

00:13:06.328 --> 00:13:08.871
Like, I used to get bashed
by professional skaters.

00:13:08.871 --> 00:13:13.493
Because my style was dorky,
or it was all circus.

00:13:13.493 --> 00:13:15.578
It was, you know?

00:13:15.578 --> 00:13:17.776
But you can't; you've got
to believe in yourself.

00:13:17.776 --> 00:13:19.955
- A lot of people come
to me and say, well,

00:13:19.955 --> 00:13:21.212
I want to be an entrepreneur.

00:13:21.212 --> 00:13:22.633
And I go, oh, that's
great; what's your idea?

00:13:22.633 --> 00:13:24.415
And they go, well, I don't have one yet.

00:13:24.415 --> 00:13:26.468
And I say, well, I think
you should go get a job

00:13:26.468 --> 00:13:28.538
as a busboy or something until
you find something you're

00:13:28.538 --> 00:13:31.560
really passionate about,
because it's a lot of work,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.622
and I'm convinced that
about half of what separates

00:13:34.622 --> 00:13:37.154
the successful entrepreneurs from the

00:13:37.154 --> 00:13:40.504
non-successful ones is pure perseverance.

00:13:40.504 --> 00:13:43.383
It is so hard, you pour
so much of your life

00:13:43.383 --> 00:13:45.398
into this thing, there
are such rough moments

00:13:45.398 --> 00:13:47.886
in time, that most people give up.

00:13:47.886 --> 00:13:49.649
I don't blame them; it's really tough.

00:13:49.649 --> 00:13:51.445
And it consumes your life, I mean,

00:13:51.445 --> 00:13:55.050
if you've got a family and
you're in the early days

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:58.825
of a company, it's, I can't
imagine how one could do it.

00:13:58.825 --> 00:14:01.435
I'm sure it's been done, but it's rough.

00:14:01.435 --> 00:14:03.794
I mean, because it's a
pretty much a, you know,

00:14:03.794 --> 00:14:06.300
an 18 hour a day job, seven
days a week for a while.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:09.572
So, unless you have a lot
of passion about this,

00:14:09.572 --> 00:14:12.881
you're going to not survive,
you're going to give it up.

00:14:12.881 --> 00:14:16.964
So you got to have an idea
or a problem or a wrong

00:14:17.906 --> 00:14:20.926
that you want to right that
you're passionate about.

00:14:20.926 --> 00:14:23.592
Otherwise, you're not going
to have the perseverance

00:14:23.592 --> 00:14:25.514
to stick it through, and I think

00:14:25.514 --> 00:14:27.242
that's half the battle, right there.

00:14:27.242 --> 00:14:29.620
It's hard to remember how bad it was,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.107
you know, in 19, early '80s,
with IBM taking over the world

00:14:33.107 --> 00:14:35.232
with the PC, with DOS out there,

00:14:35.232 --> 00:14:38.314
it was far worse than the Apple II.

00:14:38.314 --> 00:14:40.414
They tried to copy the
Apple II and they'd done

00:14:40.414 --> 00:14:43.384
a pretty bad job, and
you needed to know a lot.

00:14:43.384 --> 00:14:45.592
And so things were kind
of slipping backwards,

00:14:45.592 --> 00:14:49.073
and Macintosh was, you
saw the 1984 commercial.

00:14:49.073 --> 00:14:51.610
You know, I hope you have
that in your archives.

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:55.188
You know, Macintosh was basically this,

00:14:55.188 --> 00:14:58.521
this relatively small company, you know,

00:14:59.792 --> 00:15:03.395
in Cupertino, California,
taking on the Goliath, IBM,

00:15:03.395 --> 00:15:07.535
and saying: wait a minute,
you're way is wrong.

00:15:07.535 --> 00:15:09.316
This is not the way we
want computers to go.

00:15:09.316 --> 00:15:11.054
This is not the legacy we want to leave.

00:15:11.054 --> 00:15:13.213
This is not what we want
our kids to be learning.

00:15:13.213 --> 00:15:15.041
This is wrong, and we are going to

00:15:15.041 --> 00:15:17.652
show you the right way to do it.

00:15:17.652 --> 00:15:19.392
And here it is: it's called Macintosh.

00:15:19.392 --> 00:15:24.166
And this is so much better
that it's going to beat you.

00:15:24.166 --> 00:15:26.107
And we are going to do it.

00:15:26.107 --> 00:15:28.969
And that's what Apple stood for.

00:15:28.969 --> 00:15:30.124
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:30.124 --> 00:15:31.793
I'd love to know: what did you think?

00:15:31.793 --> 00:15:33.742
What rule stood out the most for you?

00:15:33.742 --> 00:15:35.714
What are you going to immediately apply

00:15:35.714 --> 00:15:37.535
to your life or to your business?

00:15:37.535 --> 00:15:38.895
Leave it down in the comment below,

00:15:38.895 --> 00:15:40.435
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:15:40.435 --> 00:15:42.090
I also want to give a
quick shout-out to Sumi,

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.087
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:15:44.087 --> 00:15:46.496
Your One Word, and sharing that picture

00:15:46.496 --> 00:15:49.336
of how your one word is "peace"
and what it means to you.

00:15:49.336 --> 00:15:51.278
I really appreciate the support,

00:15:51.278 --> 00:15:53.876
and I'm glad that the book
had an impact on your life.

00:15:53.876 --> 00:15:55.354
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:15:55.354 --> 00:15:57.603
I believe in you; I hope
you continue to believe

00:15:57.603 --> 00:16:00.039
in yourself, and whatever
your one word is.

00:16:00.039 --> 00:16:02.741
Much love; have an amazing day.

00:16:02.741 --> 00:16:04.241
I'll see you soon.

